# So which ones should you *actually* test?

I would **not** run an experiment with all 24. You'd produce a massive pile of results without answering a useful research question.

I'd use **six models**:

| Model                | Why include it                    |
| -------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| **GPT-5.6 Terra**    | Strong closed-model baseline      |
| **Claude Sonnet 5**  | Independent frontier closed model |
| **Gemini 3.7 Flash** | Cheap frontier API model          |
| **Mistral Small 4**  | Strong open-source MoE            |
| **Qwen3.6-35B-A3B**  | Strong math-oriented open MoE     |
| **Gemma 4 26B A4B**  | Efficient Google open model       |

And I would add **Llama 4 Maverick** if your supervisor wants Llama represented because it is such a well-known open-weight family.

That gives you a genuinely interesting comparison:

> **GPT vs Claude vs Gemini vs Mistral vs Qwen vs Gemma/Llama**

while keeping the **RAG pipeline, system prompt, retrieved documents, tool access and evaluation questions identical**.

### My current hierarchy for your exact use case

**Best raw performance candidates:**
GPT-5.6 Sol / Claude Opus 5

**Best practical closed models:**
**GPT-5.6 Terra / Claude Sonnet 5 / Gemini 3.7 Flash**

**Best open models:**
**Mistral Small 4 / Qwen3.6-35B-A3B / Gemma 4 26B or 31B**

**Best small self-hosted experimental models:**
Gemma 4 12B / Ministral 14B

**Models I would not choose as the final DSP tutor unless testing proves me wrong:**
GPT-5.6 Luna / Gemini Flash-Lite / Ministral 3B or 8B.

The really interesting dissertation question therefore isn't necessarily **“Which LLM is best?”** It is closer to:

> **Can a relatively small open-weight model, when grounded in course-specific material and given mathematical verification tools, approach the tutoring performance of a frontier proprietary model?**

That gives you a much stronger reason to investigate Mistral, Qwen, Gemma and Llama rather than simply plugging GPT into a web interface.

